.\" -*- coding: UTF-8 -*-
.\" mpmtr-hop-snmp(1). Keep in sync with cmd/mpmtr-hop-snmp/main.go.
.TH MPMTR\-HOP\-SNMP 1 "November 2026" "mpmtr" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
mpmtr\-hop\-snmp \- hop-information plugin for mpmtr: router name, interface and its errors over SNMP
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B mpmtr \-\-hop\-info
.RB \(dq snmp
.RB [ \-\-descr ]
.RB [ \-\-errors ]
.IR FILE \(dq
.RI [ options ]
.IR target [: port ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B mpmtr\-hop\-snmp
is a plugin of
.BR mpmtr (1).
It is not run by hand:
.B mpmtr \-\-hop\-info
starts it, gives it the address of every hop shown, and shows what the
plugin answers.
.PP
For a hop address the plugin asks the router at that address, over SNMP:
.IP \(bu 2
its name
.RB ( sysName ),
shown as the hop's host name instead of the reverse-DNS one;
.IP \(bu 2
the interface that owns the address
.RB ( ifName ),
shown in the
.B PLUGIN
column;
.IP \(bu 2
with
.BR \-\-descr ,
that interface's description
.RB ( ifAlias );
.IP \(bu 2
with
.BR \-\-errors ,
that interface's receive errors.
.PP
.EX
HOST                        ...  PLUGIN
core1.example (192.0.2.1)   ...  Ethernet1/2     «to spine 3»  crc:0
core2.example (192.0.2.5)   ...  Port\-Channel37  «uplink»      err:12 +3
.EE
.PP
Hop addresses come from the network, not from you: a router anywhere on the
path chooses the address it answers with.
So the plugin asks nothing unless
.I FILE
allows that very address; see
.BR CONFIGURATION .
.SH OPTIONS
The plugin's options go inside the quotes of
.BR \-\-hop\-info ,
before the file name.
.TP
.B \-\-descr
Show the interface's description
.RB ( ifAlias )
after its name, in «».
.TP
.B \-\-errors
Show the interface's receive errors after that; see
.BR "ERROR COUNTERS" .
.TP
.I FILE
The configuration: which addresses may be asked and how.
Required.
.SH CONFIGURATION
One line per prefix: the prefix (or a single address), then
.IR key = value
pairs separated by spaces.
.B #
starts a comment.
.PP
.EX
# SNMPv2c: the community travels in clear text to every address asked
10.0.0.0/8      community=public
# SNMPv3 with authentication and encryption
192.0.2.0/24    user=monitor auth=sha256:secret\-one priv=aes:secret\-two
# another port and patience for one slow device
10.1.2.3        community=public port=1161 timeout=3s retries=0
.EE
.TP
.BI community= TEXT
SNMPv2c with this community.
.TP
.BI user= NAME
SNMPv3 with this user.
Alone it means no authentication and no encryption.
.TP
.BI auth= PROTOCOL : PASSPHRASE
SNMPv3 authentication:
.BR md5 ,
.BR sha ,
.BR sha224 ,
.BR sha256 ,
.BR sha384 ,
.BR sha512 .
.TP
.BI priv= PROTOCOL : PASSPHRASE
SNMPv3 encryption:
.BR des ,
.BR aes ,
.BR aes192 ,
.BR aes256 .
Needs
.BR auth .
.TP
.BI port= N
UDP port of the agent (default 161).
.TP
.BI timeout= D
How long to wait for one answer (default 1s, at most 10s).
.TP
.BI retries= N
How many times a request is repeated after a timeout (default 1, 0\-5).
.PP
Rules:
.IP \(bu 2
The longest matching prefix wins.
.IP \(bu 2
An address in no prefix is never sent a packet.
.IP \(bu 2
A line has either
.B community
or
.BR user ,
not both.
.IP \(bu 2
Passphrases are at least 8 characters and have no spaces (a value ends at
the first space).
.IP \(bu 2
The file must not be readable by group or others
.RB ( "chmod 600" ):
it holds credentials, and the plugin refuses a file that is.
.IP \(bu 2
A file with no prefixes is an error: nothing would ever be asked.
.PP
On any error in the file the plugin prints the line number and what is
wrong to standard error and exits.
.SH WHAT IS SENT
For one address, at most two SNMP GET requests, both to that address.
No table is walked.
SNMPv3 adds the exchange in which the device tells its engine ID.
.IP 1. 4
.B sysName.0
and the interface index of the address:
.B ipAddressIfIndex
(the table for IPv4 and IPv6) and, for an IPv4 address, also
.B ipAdEntIfIndex
(the older IPv4 table, for devices that fill only that one).
.IP 2. 4
If an interface index came back:
.BR ifName ,
with
.B \-\-descr
also
.BR ifAlias ,
with
.B \-\-errors
also
.B dot3StatsFCSErrors
and
.BR ifInErrors ,
all for that index, in one request.
.PP
If the device knows its name and not the interface of the address, only the
name is shown.
If it does not answer, nothing is shown for that address; nothing else
changes.
.PP
The plugin asks at most 8 addresses at a time.
One request waits
.B timeout
and is repeated
.B retries
times, so a silent address costs up to timeout \(mu (retries + 1): 2 s by
default.
.PP
How often an address is asked is decided by
.BR mpmtr :
once, when the hop is first shown.
With
.BI \-\-hop\-info\-interval " D"
an address still on the screen is asked again when its answer is that old.
The old answer stays until the new one is in, and a new answer that says
nothing does not erase it.
.SH ERROR COUNTERS
With
.B \-\-errors
the note ends with one of:
.TP
.BI crc: N
Frames received with a bad checksum
.RB ( dot3StatsFCSErrors ).
Shown when the interface has this counter: a physical Ethernet port.
.TP
.BI err: N
All input errors together
.RB ( ifInErrors ).
Shown when the interface has no checksum counter: a port-channel, a VLAN
interface.
.TP
nothing
The device gives neither.
.PP
The counters run since the device booted (or since they were cleared), so
the number alone says little.
When
.B mpmtr
asks again
.RB ( \-\-hop\-info\-interval ),
the plugin adds the growth since its own first look at that address:
.RS
.EX
crc:1532 +12
.EE
.RE
.PP
means 1532 in total, 12 of them since the plugin first asked.
A counter that went back (the device restarted, the counters were cleared)
starts the count anew.
Without
.B \-\-hop\-info\-interval
there is no second look and only the total is shown.
.SH WHICH INTERFACE
The interface is the one that owns the address the router answered from.
That is usually the interface the probe came in through, but a router may
answer from another interface or from a loopback.
The interface the traffic leaves through is not told.
.PP
A hop on a port-channel or a VLAN interface shows the errors of that
logical interface, not of the physical ports under it.
.SH EXAMPLES
Names and interfaces:
.RS
.EX
mpmtr \-\-hop\-info "snmp /etc/mpmtr/snmp.conf" example.com
.EE
.RE
.PP
With descriptions and errors, refreshed every minute:
.RS
.EX
mpmtr \-\-hop\-info "snmp \-\-descr \-\-errors /etc/mpmtr/snmp.conf" \e
      \-\-hop\-info\-interval 60s example.com
.EE
.RE
.PP
Together with another plugin (their notes stand side by side):
.RS
.EX
mpmtr \-\-hop\-info "snmp /etc/mpmtr/snmp.conf" \-\-hop\-info rack example.com
.EE
.RE
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
.B mpmtr
closed the plugin's input.
.TP
.B 1
The configuration file cannot be read, is readable by others, or has an
error.
.TP
.B 2
Wrong command line.
.SH FILES
.B mpmtr
looks for the executable
.B mpmtr\-hop\-snmp
next to itself, then in
.BR PATH .
.SH SECURITY
.IP \(bu 2
An SNMPv2c community travels in clear text to every address the file
allows.
Keep the prefixes as narrow as your own network.
.IP \(bu 2
A prefix that covers addresses you do not control hands them the
community: whoever answers from such an address on the path gets a request
with it.
.IP \(bu 2
.B mpmtr
itself sends nothing for this and has no SNMP options: the credentials live
only in the file.
.IP \(bu 2
The plugin only reads (GET); it never writes to a device.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR mpmtr (1),
.BR mpmtr\-app\-http (1),
.BR snmpget (1)
.PP
IF-MIB (RFC 2863), IP-MIB (RFC 4293), EtherLike-MIB (RFC 3635).
.PP
.B docs/PLUGINS.md
(the plugin protocol),
.B docs/WRITING_PLUGINS.md
(how to write a plugin).
